+const targetPrograms=programs.filter(p=>/PS/i.test(p.materials));
+const seedText=`My interest in telecommunications began with a fictional undergraduate project about monitoring signal quality across a small campus network. Our first prototype worked only when the devices were close together. I became curious about what happened between a transmitted signal and the information received at the other end. Instead of treating the dropped packets as a simple coding error, I started comparing the effect of distance, interference and antenna position. That experience made communication systems feel like a connected engineering problem: the performance of a network depends on the choices made at every layer.
+
+My undergraduate studies have given me a foundation in electronic engineering, mathematics and programming. In a fictional Signals and Systems module, I learned to represent continuous and discrete signals and to interpret frequency-domain behaviour. A digital communications class introduced modulation, channel effects and error control. I have also used Python to organise experimental data and visualise changes between test conditions. These experiences taught me to check assumptions before drawing conclusions. When a result looked unexpected, I returned to the measurement setup, repeated the test and recorded the conditions rather than selecting only the data that supported my first idea.
+
+For a fictional wireless sensor project, my team built a basic prototype to send temperature readings between two rooms. I was responsible for comparing packet loss at different distances and documenting the test procedure. The first set of results varied because the receiver was moved between trials. I suggested fixing its position, repeating each measurement and logging the surrounding conditions. The revised tests were more consistent and helped us distinguish a weak signal from an inconsistent method. I contributed the measurement plan and analysis, while the circuit and software were developed collaboratively. The project strengthened my interest in reliable communication and showed me how practical validation can improve a technically simple system.
+
+I am applying to the Telecommunications MSc at UCL because I want to study communication systems and networks in greater depth. The programme’s coverage of communication technologies, network design and data networks is relevant to the questions that emerged from my project. I would like to connect theoretical understanding with the evaluation of real systems, particularly how design decisions affect reliability and performance. Before submitting, I will verify the current module list and explain which parts best support my goals. I am also interested in learning with students whose technical backgrounds and experiences differ from mine, because network problems benefit from more than one perspective.
+
+After the degree, my short-term goal is to begin in a communications or network engineering role where I can contribute to testing, performance analysis and system development. Over time, I hope to take responsibility for designing more dependable connected systems. I know that I still need stronger preparation in advanced communication theory and network architecture. Postgraduate study would give me a structured opportunity to build that knowledge and practise applying it. My project experience has not made me an expert, but it has shown me how I learn: define a measurable question, make a careful test, and revise my explanation when the evidence changes. I hope to bring that approach to further study and future engineering work.
+
+I believe that this course will be a very important step in my life. UCL is an excellent university with a strong reputation, and I am confident that studying there will allow me to achieve my dreams. I am hardworking, passionate and motivated to make a positive contribution to society. I look forward to the opportunity to learn from leading academics and to develop the skills required for a successful career in the modern world.`;
